\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {S8}{\ignorespaces Premise test under the label-permutation null. The statistic is the mean over compounds of (agreement with itself across concentrations) minus (agreement with the other compounds' consensus patterns). Fixing the first concentration's labels leaves $(n!)^{C-1}$ distinct relabellings, which is enumerable for the four-compound set and sampled for the others.}}{18}{table.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{tab:premisenull}{{S8}{18}{Premise test under the label-permutation null. The statistic is the mean over compounds of (agreement with itself across concentrations) minus (agreement with the other compounds' consensus patterns). Fixing the first concentration's labels leaves $(n!)^{C-1}$ distinct relabellings, which is enumerable for the four-compound set and sampled for the others}{table.8}{}}
\@writefile{toc}{\contentsline {section}{\numberline {S21}The measured safety axis, and the holes in it}{20}{section.21}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{res:safety}{{S21}{20}{The measured safety axis, and the holes in it}{section.21}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {S4}{\ignorespaces The second half of the criterion, measured and then modelled. (a) EPA AEGL-3 at 10~min on a logarithmic scale for the nineteen compounds that have one; the incumbent simulant dichloromethane sits near the safe end but is not the safest, methanol and n-hexane both scoring higher. Five of the nineteen values come from the agency's own footnotes rather than from the table cell, because they exceed half the lower explosive limit. (b) The nine compounds with no regulator-grade acute inhalation value, with the reason for each; DMMP, which supplies every valid match on the twenty-eight-compound panel and zero on the primary one, is among them. (c) Out-of-fold predictions for the 230 training chemicals (orange) with the twenty military agents held out of training (red) overlaid; arrows run from the measured value to the prediction, and every agent is predicted safer than it is. Labels name the ten agents of this paper's panel that the AEGL set also holds and the three nitrogen mustards; the remaining seven held-out agents are plotted unlabelled. (d) Prediction error against applicability-domain distance. Every one of the twenty is wrong by between 0.40 and 3.42 log units, and fourteen of them pass the domain check, the worst of those fourteen being wrong by 2.68 log units. The dotted line marks where DMMP sits.}}{21}{figure.4}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:tox}{{S4}{21}{The second half of the criterion, measured and then modelled. (a) EPA AEGL-3 at 10~min on a logarithmic scale for the nineteen compounds that have one; the incumbent simulant dichloromethane sits near the safe end but is not the safest, methanol and n-hexane both scoring higher. Five of the nineteen values come from the agency's own footnotes rather than from the table cell, because they exceed half the lower explosive limit. (b) The nine compounds with no regulator-grade acute inhalation value, with the reason for each; DMMP, which supplies every valid match on the twenty-eight-compound panel and zero on the primary one, is among them. (c) Out-of-fold predictions for the 230 training chemicals (orange) with the twenty military agents held out of training (red) overlaid; arrows run from the measured value to the prediction, and every agent is predicted safer than it is. Labels name the ten agents of this paper's panel that the AEGL set also holds and the three nitrogen mustards; the remaining seven held-out agents are plotted unlabelled. (d) Prediction error against applicability-domain distance. Every one of the twenty is wrong by between 0.40 and 3.42 log units, and fourteen of them pass the domain check, the worst of those fourteen being wrong by 2.68 log units. The dotted line marks where DMMP sits}{figure.4}{}}
